  "problem": "A certain company produces two types of footwear: casual and sports. The following table describes how the number of customers changes between both types of footwear each year:\n\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td colspan=\"2\" align=\"center\">From:</td> \n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td></td>\n<td></td>\n<td width=\"80\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\"><b>Casual</b></td>\n<td width=\"80\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\"><b>Sports</b></td> \n</tr>\n<tr> \n<td rowspan=\"2\" align=\"center\">To:</td>\n<td width=\"80\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\"><b>Casual</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.55$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.7$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr> \n<td width=\"80\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" align=\"center\"><b>Sports</b></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.45$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.3$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\nThat is, $45%$ of casual customers switch to sports each year, and $70%$ of sports customers change to casual.\n\nCurrently, $700\\,000$ customers purchase casual footwear, while $300\\,000$ customers purchase sports footwear. How many customers will purchase sports footwear in one year? You may assume that the total number of customers doesn't change.",
